## Functions That Modify In Place

Use `&mut` when a function needs to modify data it receives:

```xiom
fn normalize(scores: &mut Vec[Int]) {
  let m = max_val(scores);  // Valid: &Vec coerces from &mut Vec
  let i = 0;
  while i < scores.len() {
    scores[i] = scores[i] * 100 / m;
    i = i + 1;
  }
}

fn clamp(scores: &mut Vec[Int], lo: Int, hi: Int)
  requires: lo <= hi;
{
  let i = 0;
  while i < scores.len() {
    if scores[i] < lo { scores[i] = lo; }
    if scores[i] > hi { scores[i] = hi; }
    i = i + 1;
  }
}
```

### Exclusive Access Guarantee

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var data = Vec[Int].new();
  data.push(10);
  data.push(50);
  data.push(30);

  normalize(&mut data);  // Exclusive write — no other access
  clamp(&mut data, 0, 100);  // Another exclusive write
  // Between normalize and clamp, data is available again
  return data[0];
}
```

### &mut Coerces to & for Reads

```xiom
fn process(data: &mut Vec[Int]) {
  let len = data.len();  // Auto-coerces to & for read
  let first = data[0];   // Also auto-coerces
  data.push(99);          // Uses &mut for write
}
```

The compiler allows re-borrowing `&mut` as `&` temporarily when you only need to read.

